{
 "$comment": "Every verdict a deliverable can receive, and what this package knows about it. HAND-MAINTAINED for `family` and `since`; `checker` and `severity` are held to the checkers themselves by check_repo's `gate declarations` guard, so this file cannot lie about what gates. WHY IT EXISTS: a gate used to be a tuple in a function body classified by whether its display string contained the substring '(gates)'. Four readers parsed that string with three different rules, and three rows were misclassified in production - M4zh_banned_hits gated in check_prose's exit and was invisible to gating.py (the regex (M\\d+)_ cannot match M4zh_), while D37_caption_name_len and D38_agenda_run_echo said 'reported' in their own targets and were counted as gates by every consumer, because gating.py keyed on the D\\d+ PREFIX. FIELDS: `family` is the concept the verdict belongs to - the classification that was missing while the set grew one verdict at a time. `since` is the release that introduced it, read from git; 'always' means it predates the version history this CHANGELOG keeps (the old 1.6.0-3.3.0 scheme sorts above 0.1.560 and would have silenced six gates if written as a number). `privacy_terms` is the fiftieth gate and had no registry entry anywhere: check_privacy reports one `verdict` per FILE rather than a verdicts map, so it fits no row table, and `check_deliverable` promotes it to the gating bucket in code. It failed builds while `gating.py`, this register and `run_conformance`'s require set all had no idea it existed. `na_means` is present on a gate whose `n/a` means the predicate had nothing to look at, and absent on one whose `n/a` means it could not be measured — a distinction no checker holds, and the reason check_design's blind-gate rule could not tell a Chinese-only metric on an English deck from a commercial gate gone silent on unreadable markup. A document declaring an older build version is NOT held to a later gate - it reports `not held`, which is neither a pass nor a failure.",
